  "rootCause": "A failure of the protocol to verify whether the NFT had actually been transferred when users used a specified NFT as collateral for borrowing. Vulnerable code snippet: Attack tx: https://etherscan.io/tx/0x8d3036371ccf27579d3cb3d4b4b71e99334cae8d7e8088247517ec640c7a59a5 Analysis: https://blog.solidityscan.com/pawnfi-hack-analysis-38ac9160cbb4",
  "descriptions": [
    {
      "source": "DeFiHackLabs",
      "text": "Business Logic Flaw. Lost: ~$820K"
    },
    {
      "source": "DeFiHackLabs explorer",
      "text": "A failure of the protocol to verify whether the NFT had actually been transferred when users used a specified NFT as collateral for borrowing. Vulnerable code snippet: Attack tx: https://etherscan.io/tx/0x8d3036371ccf27579d3cb3d4b4b71e99334cae8d7e8088247517ec640c7a59a5 Analysis: https://blog.solidityscan.com/pawnfi-hack-analysis-38ac9160cbb4"
    },
    {
      "source": "RevertIQ",
      "text": "Pawnfi suffered an $820,000 exploit on Ethereum Mainnet on June 17, 2023, when the protocol failed to verify NFT collateral transfers. The attacker borrowed APE tokens using NFTs that were already staked, enabling repeated borrow and withdrawal operations. Pawnfi's smart contract logic allowed borrowing against NFTs without confirming ownership transfer, enabling an attacker to collateralize already-staked NFTs and withdraw tokens fraudulently."
    }
  ],
